ously digital camp  cameras that capture the image digitally  but also the the digital chip that is  recording these is getting bigger and  bigger and so we're dealing with image  density pixel density that's huge  compared to what it was even five years  ago or certainly 10 years ago what this  allows us to do  editorially is recompose the image  within the frame and it's very useful in  a documentary because when you're  shooting a documentary you don't know  where this particular moment is going to  go in the film how it's going to  integrate and so there may be things  within the frame that ultimately will  not fit within the story and so the  ability to recompose to to enlarge the  frame and omit something that is now  incorrect  perhaps a character who has disappeared  from the film is in this shot well we  can just eliminate that character by  narrowing the field and we used to not  be able to do that so freely because a  it was expensive and be even if you  could do it the the grain structure of  film was such that it now you knew  something had happened to this shot  whereas with digital film it's not clear  that that's the case so we can in a  sense photograph the film a second time  in the editing by recomposing within the  within the frame in a sense if you if  you think of normal editing it's  horizontal one shot after the other like  a row of cars and a train the this kind  of editing I'm talking about is vertical  editing where you're editing within the  frame and choosing to see or not see  certain things in the original  photography  well I think the best thing you can do  as an editor who is trying to learn how  to edit is to edit and in that sense  people today are in a much better  position than we were 50 years ago  because then film was very expensive and  the machines on which we could edit film  were very expensive and it was just hard  to find the time and the resources to to  get your hands on the stuff now the  Internet is just overloaded 